---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ArticleHeader from "../components/ArticleHeader.astro";
import TableOfContents from "../components/TableOfContents.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { AUTHORS, DEFAULT_AUTHOR, authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from "../data/site";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Clear Protein Powder",
    "url": "/best-clear-protein-powder/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Lactose-Free Protein Powder",
    "url": "/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/"
  }
];
const author = AUTHORS[DEFAULT_AUTHOR];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-20", modified: "2026-11-20" });
const title = "Ryse vs Ghost Protein: Which Licensed-Flavor Whey Wins?";
const description =
  "Ryse Loaded wins on price: about $1.67 per 25 g vs $2.12 for Ghost Whey, both 25 g protein. Ghost wins on enzymes, soy-free label and a single sweetener.";

const PRODUCTS: {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  brand?: string;
  badge: string;
  category: string;
  description: string;
  pros: string[];
  cons: string[];
  bottomLine: string;
  specs?: Record<string, string>;
  rating?: number;
  reviewCount?: number;
  amazonUrl?: string;
}[] = [
  {
    "id": "ryse-vs-ghost-protein-ryse-loaded",
    "name": "RYSE Loaded Protein, Jet-Puffed Marshmallow (2 lb)",
    "brand": "RYSE",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "Whey isolate and concentrate blend",
    "description": "Ryse Loaded Protein gives 25 g of protein in a 33.9 g scoop with about 130 calories, 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 2.5 g of fat in the Jet-Puffed Marshmallow flavor, per the label at the time of writing; figures vary by flavor. The protein comes from whey isolate and whey concentrate, with added MCT powder and organic agave inulin, sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. A 2 lb tub holds about 27 servings.",
    "pros": [
      "About $1.67 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw",
      "Slightly higher protein density than Ghost Whey (about 74% vs 71%)",
      "Large licensed lineup: Jet-Puffed, Skippy, Little Debbie and more",
      "4 lb tubs bring the cost down further"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Two artificial sweeteners (sucralose and acesulfame potassium)",
      "Some flavors list soy lecithin and carrageenan",
      "No sport certification claimed or found"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheaper of the two for the same 25 g of protein, with the bigger licensed flavor list.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g in 33.9 g (label, varies by flavor)",
      "Calories": "About 130",
      "Third-party testing": "No sport certification found",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $1.67 ($44.99, 27 servings, Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BDTMHBC8"
  },
  {
    "id": "ryse-vs-ghost-protein-ghost-whey",
    "name": "GHOST Whey Protein, Cereal Milk (2 lb)",
    "brand": "GHOST",
    "badge": "Best for Sensitive Stomachs",
    "category": "Whey isolate, concentrate and hydrolyzed isolate blend",
    "description": "Ghost Whey gives 25 g of protein in a 35 g rounded scoop with about 130 calories, 4 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar and 1.5 g of fat in Milk Chocolate, per the label at the time of writing; figures vary by flavor. The protein is a blend of whey isolate, whey concentrate and hydrolyzed whey isolate, with a digestive enzyme blend that includes lactase, sweetened with sucralose only. Ghost labels it soy-free and gluten-free.",
    "pros": [
      "Digestive enzymes including lactase and bromelain",
      "Only one artificial sweetener (sucralose)",
      "Soy-free and gluten-free, per Ghost",
      "Licensed cereal-milk flavors such as Lucky Charms and Cocoa Puffs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $2.12 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw",
      "Fewer servings per tub in some flavors (26 vs 27-28)",
      "No sport certification claimed or found"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pricier pick, worth it if lactase, a soy-free label or a single sweetener matters to you.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g in 35 g (label, varies by flavor)",
      "Calories": "About 130",
      "Third-party testing": "No sport certification found",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.12 ($54.99, 26 servings, Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B01N9BO3SE"
  },
  {
    "id": "ryse-vs-ghost-protein-dymatize-iso100",
    "name": "Dymatize ISO100 Hydrolyzed Whey Isolate, Fruity Pebbles (3 lb)",
    "brand": "Dymatize",
    "badge": "Certified Alternative",
    "category": "Hydrolyzed whey isolate and whey isolate",
    "description": "If neither brand's lack of certification works for you, ISO100 is the licensed-cereal-flavor whey with a certification: Dymatize states it carries Informed Choice. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop gives 25 g of protein from hydrolyzed whey isolate and whey isolate, with very little fat, carbohydrate or lactose. It comes in Fruity Pebbles and Cocoa Pebbles, and costs about the same per gram as Ghost.",
    "pros": [
      "Informed Choice certification, per Dymatize",
      "100% isolate, very low in lactose",
      "Licensed Fruity Pebbles and Cocoa Pebbles flavors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $2.14 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw",
      "Thinner texture than the Ryse and Ghost blends, per buyer reviews"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The swap for drug-tested athletes who still want a cereal flavor.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 110-120",
      "Third-party testing": "Informed Choice (per Dymatize)",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.14 ($96.20, 45 servings, Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0855PRCBV"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Ryse vs Ghost Protein",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Ryse or Ghost protein better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most buyers Ryse Loaded Protein is the better value, since it gives the same 25 g of protein per scoop for about $1.67 per 25 g against about $2.12 for Ghost Whey at the Amazon prices we saw. Ghost is better if you want added lactase, a soy-free label or only one artificial sweetener. Taste depends on which licensed flavors you prefer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Ryse or Ghost third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither claims NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice certification on its product page at the time of writing, and we did not find Ryse Loaded in those registries. That is not evidence of a problem, but drug-tested athletes should choose a powder with a verifiable certification, such as Dymatize ISO100, which Dymatize says is Informed Choice certified."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which has more protein, Ryse or Ghost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are tied at 25 g of protein per scoop, per the labels at the time of writing. Ryse fits it into a 33.9 g scoop and Ghost into a 35 g scoop, so Ryse is slightly denser at about 74 percent protein by weight against 71 percent. Some flavors of each differ by a gram, so check the panel on your tub."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Ghost or Ryse protein have lactose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both contain lactose, because both include whey concentrate alongside isolate. Ghost adds a digestive enzyme blend with lactase, which may help people with mild lactose sensitivity, though the dose is not disclosed. Ryse does not list enzymes on the labels we read. People with real lactose intolerance should consider a whey isolate, clear whey or plant protein instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which tastes better, Ryse or Ghost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers rate both highly, and the better one depends on the flavor you want. Ghost is known for cereal-milk flavors such as Lucky Charms, Cocoa Puffs and Trix. Ryse is known for snack flavors such as Jet-Puffed Marshmallow, Skippy Peanut Butter and Little Debbie. We have not tasted either; this reflects buyer reviews. Single-serve samples are the cheapest way to decide."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Ryse or Ghost protein good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both can fit a weight-loss diet: each gives 25 g of protein in about 130 calories, or roughly 5.2 calories per gram of protein. Neither is meaningfully leaner than the other. Mixing in water instead of milk saves more calories than switching brands, and a clear whey isolate gives similar protein in about 100 calories if you want something lighter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are Ryse and Ghost protein safe?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both are mainstream whey powders sold by major retailers and are generally fine for healthy adults at one or two scoops a day. Both contain milk, Ryse lists soy in some flavors, and both use artificial sweeteners. People with kidney disease, allergies, digestive conditions, pregnancy or regular medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using a protein supplement."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Comparisons",
    tags: [
      "ryse vs ghost protein",
      "ghost vs ryse protein",
      "ryse loaded protein vs ghost whey",
      "ryse protein",
      "ghost whey protein",
      "best tasting protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Best-Tasting Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-tasting-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Head-to-Head"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Ryse vs Ghost Protein</Fragment>
      Two licensed-flavor whey blends with the same 25 g of protein per scoop. Here is how RYSE Loaded Protein and Ghost Whey differ on price, ingredients, stomach comfort and what buyers say about taste.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#protein-per-scoop">Protein per Scoop</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">What Is in Each</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Flavors</a>
      <a href="#stomach">Stomach Comfort</a>
      <a href="#testing">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#goals">By Goal</a>
      <a href="#other-ranges">Clear and Vegan Ranges</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Ryse Loaded Protein is the better buy for most people: it gives the same 25 g of protein per scoop as Ghost Whey for about 21 percent less per gram at the Amazon prices we saw. Ghost is the pick if you want digestive enzymes, a soy-free label or only one artificial sweetener. Below we compare labels, cost per 25 g, testing, buyer-reported taste and each brand's other ranges.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Ryse vs Ghost Protein: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse Loaded Protein and Ghost Whey both give 25 g of protein in about 130 calories per scoop, per their labels at the time of writing, so the choice comes down to price, the extras on the label and which licensed flavors you want. At the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026, Ryse costs about $1.67 per 25 g of protein and Ghost about $2.12. Neither claims a sport certification.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RYSE Loaded Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">GHOST Whey</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g in 33.9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g in 35 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g / 2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, whey concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, concentrate, hydrolyzed isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame potassium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extras</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">MCT powder, agave inulin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Digestive enzymes incl. lactase</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sport certification</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g (Amazon, 20 Nov)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.67</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.12</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where sold</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, Vitacost, The Vitamin Shoppe, brand site</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Amazon (Ghost's official store), brand site</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Label figures are for Ryse Jet-Puffed Marshmallow and Ghost Milk Chocolate; both brands' panels change from flavor to flavor. Where sold reflects the retailers listing each brand at the time of writing, and stock varies by store.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick Ryse if you want the lower price; pick Ghost if your stomach or your label preferences decide it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0"><strong>Pick Ryse if</strong> you want the same 25 g of protein for less, prefer snack and dessert flavors like Skippy or Little Debbie, or buy 4 lb tubs. <strong>Pick Ghost if</strong> regular whey bloats you and you want added lactase, you avoid soy or acesulfame potassium, or you want cereal-milk flavors. <strong>Pick neither if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete: choose a certified whey such as Dymatize ISO100.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ryse Loaded Protein and Ghost Whey Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            One card per side, plus a certified alternative with licensed cereal flavors for drug-tested athletes. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026 and will move.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="protein-per-scoop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Ryse or Ghost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse and Ghost tie at 25 g of protein per scoop, per their labels at the time of writing. Ryse Loaded fits that 25 g into a 33.9 g scoop of Jet-Puffed Marshmallow, while Ghost Whey uses a 35 g rounded scoop of Milk Chocolate. That makes Ryse very slightly denser: about 74 percent protein by weight against about 71 percent for Ghost.
          </p>

          <p>
            A three-point gap in density is too small to matter at the shaker. It does explain why Ryse scores a little higher on our <a href="/methodology/">Label Score</a>, where protein density carries the most weight in the Label Score: Ryse Loaded scores 56 out of 100 and Ghost Whey 48. The rest of the gap comes from cost per gram and from Ghost's longer list of flagged additives such as cellulose gum and artificial flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger caveat is flavor. Both brands sell dozens of flavors, and the scoop size moves with each one because cookie pieces, marshmallows and candy chips add weight without adding protein. Ryse listings show scoops from about 30 g to just over 37 g depending on flavor, and some Ghost flavors are listed with 26 g of protein instead of 25 g. Read the panel for the exact tub you buy rather than assuming the figures above.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand publishes a full amino acid profile on its product page, so we cannot compare leucine directly. Both are whey, which is naturally high in leucine, and 25 g of whey generally lands in the range most research associates with a strong muscle-building response. For protein per scoop, call it a draw and decide on the other factors below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per the label, at the time of writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein in either scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">74%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ryse protein by weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">71%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ghost protein by weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">130</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories in either</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Actually in Ryse Loaded and Ghost Whey?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both are whey blends rather than pure isolates, and the main difference is what each brand adds. Ryse Loaded lists instantized whey protein isolate first, then whey protein concentrate, then MCT powder and organic agave inulin, per the Jet-Puffed Marshmallow label at the time of writing. Ghost Whey lists a whey blend of isolate, concentrate and hydrolyzed isolate, then flavor, cocoa, salt and a digestive enzyme blend of proteases, bromelain and lactase, per the Milk Chocolate label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Ryse adds: MCTs and prebiotic fiber</h3>

          <p>
            Ryse markets its "Loaded" name on the extras. The MCT powder is a small amount of medium-chain fat carried on sodium caseinate, which is a milk protein, so Ryse is not casein-free. Agave inulin is a prebiotic fiber; the Jet-Puffed label prints dietary fiber as under 1 g, so the dose is small. Ryse's gum blend varies by flavor, and on its own site some flavors list guar gum, xanthan gum and gum acacia, while others list cellulose gum, xanthan gum and carrageenan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Ghost adds: enzymes and a disclosed whey split</h3>

          <p>
            Ghost's distinguishing extra is the enzyme blend. Lactase breaks down lactose, which matters because whey concentrate carries more lactose than isolate, and proteases help break down protein. Ghost also has a long habit of disclosing how its whey is split, and its marketing calls it a 100 percent whey protein blend. The thickeners are cellulose gum and xanthan gum, and the sweetener is sucralose alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens differ in one useful way. Both contain milk. Ghost labels Ghost Whey soy-free, while Ryse's Jet-Puffed Marshmallow lists soy as an allergen and several Ryse flavors print "soy and/or sunflower lecithin". If you avoid soy, Ghost is the simpler buy. If you avoid carrageenan, check the specific Ryse flavor before ordering. Anyone with a milk allergy should skip both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither formula is "clean" in the strict sense: both use artificial flavor and non-nutritive sweeteners, and the dessert flavors with real cookie or candy pieces add a little sugar and extra ingredients. If you want a short ingredient list, look at unflavored or stevia-sweetened powders in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Is Cheaper per 25 g of Protein, Ryse or Ghost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse is cheaper: about $1.67 per 25 g of protein against about $2.12 for Ghost, at the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026. A 2 lb tub of Ryse Loaded Jet-Puffed Marshmallow was $44.99 for 27 servings of 25 g, and a 2 lb tub of Ghost Whey Cereal Milk was $54.99 for 26 servings of 25 g. That makes Ghost about 27 percent more expensive per gram, or Ryse about 21 percent cheaper.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tub (Amazon, 20 Nov 2026)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Servings x protein</th>
