        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Tastes Better, Ryse or Ghost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers rate both highly for taste, and neither has a clear edge across the board; the honest answer is that it depends on which licensed flavor you want. We have not tasted either powder, so everything in this section is what buyers report in retailer reviews and on forums. Both brands built their names on licensed flavors, and both are regular picks in our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best-tasting protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ghost: cereal milk and dessert flavors</h3>

          <p>
            Ghost's licensed lineup leans on breakfast cereal: Lucky Charms, Cocoa Puffs and Trix cereal milks, plus Peanut Butter Cereal Milk, Cinnabon and Chocolate Chip Cookies. Ghost says its Lucky Charms flavor contains real marshmallow pieces. Buyers commonly describe Ghost's cereal-milk flavors as closely matching the milk left in a cereal bowl, and Coffee Ice Cream gets frequent mentions from people who mix protein into iced coffee.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ryse: snack-cake and candy flavors</h3>

          <p>
            Ryse's licensed lineup leans on snacks and desserts: Jet-Puffed Marshmallow, Skippy Peanut Butter and a long list of Little Debbie flavors such as Cosmic Brownies and Strawberry Shortcake Rolls, plus seasonal tubs. Several Ryse flavors include real inclusions, such as mini marshmallows, freeze-dried strawberry or candy-coated chocolate chips, per the ingredient lists. Buyers commonly praise Ryse's thick, creamy texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common complaints mirror each other. Some buyers find both brands too sweet, especially in water, and recommend milk or a half scoop. Flavors with real cookie or candy pieces can leave bits at the bottom of a shaker, so a blender helps. Flavor is personal, so the cheapest way to choose is a single-serve sample: both brands sell them through their own sites and some gyms.
