        <div id="stomach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Easier on the Stomach, Ryse or Ghost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ghost Whey is the safer bet for a sensitive stomach because it adds lactase, the enzyme that breaks down lactose, and uses only one artificial sweetener. Ryse Loaded has no enzymes listed on the labels we read, uses two sweeteners and adds inulin, a fermentable fiber. None of that guarantees comfort, but each point leans the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            The main cause of bloating from whey is lactose, and both products contain whey concentrate, which carries more lactose than isolate. Ghost's added lactase may help some people with mild lactose intolerance, though the amount is not disclosed and enzymes in a powder are not a substitute for a lactose-free product. Ryse leads with isolate, which helps, but it does not add an enzyme to break down the concentrate's share.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inulin is the other variable. It is a prebiotic fiber that gut bacteria ferment, and in sensitive people even a small amount can cause gas. The dose in Ryse Loaded appears small, since the Jet-Puffed label prints fiber as under 1 g, but if inulin bothers you in other foods it may bother you here. Sugar alcohols are not on either label we read, which removes another common cause of stomach trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are properly lactose intolerant, neither blend is the right tool. A 100 percent whey isolate, a clear whey or a plant protein is a better starting point; see our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> guide. Anyone with kidney disease, a milk allergy, a digestive condition, or who is pregnant or taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Ryse or Ghost Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither Ryse Loaded Protein nor Ghost Whey claims NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice certification on its product page at the time of writing. When we checked on 1 November 2026, Ryse Loaded was not listed in the NSF Certified for Sport registry or on the Informed Sport and Informed Choice brand lists. Ghost does not list a sport certification on its Ghost Whey page.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean either product is unsafe or mislabeled. Brands often test their own batches or use contract labs, and both companies sell through major retailers that have their own supplier requirements. It does mean there is no independent, public certificate you can look up for either tub, which is the thing drug-tested athletes and cautious buyers need.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is where the decision becomes simple for some readers. If you compete in a tested sport, buy a protein with a certification you can verify in the certifier's own database. Dymatize ISO100 is the closest match in spirit, since it also sells licensed Fruity Pebbles and Cocoa Pebbles flavors, and Dymatize states that it carries Informed Choice certification; confirm the specific flavor before relying on it. Our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification status can change, so treat this as a snapshot. Check the NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport databases yourself before buying, and be wary of any listing that claims a certification the brand does not mention on its own site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="goals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Is Better for Weight Loss, Muscle Gain or Coffee?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For weight loss and muscle gain, Ryse and Ghost are close to interchangeable, because both give 25 g of protein in about 130 calories. The deciding factor for most goals is that Ryse costs less per gram, which makes it easier to hit a daily protein target with one or two shakes without overspending.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a calorie deficit, look at grams of protein per calorie: both land at about 5.2 calories per gram of protein on the labels we compared. Ghost has 1 g less fat and Ryse 1 g less carbohydrate and sugar, which cancel out. If you want something leaner than either, a clear whey isolate or a pure isolate gives 25 g in roughly 100 to 120 calories. Mixing either in water rather than milk saves more calories than switching brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle gain, total daily protein matters far more than the brand. A common evidence-based target is roughly 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, spread over meals. One scoop of either covers 25 g of that. Neither contains creatine or added carbohydrate, so pair it with food or a separate supplement if you want those. If you are cutting the cost of a bulk, the 4 lb Ryse tub is the cheaper route at about $1.50 per 25 g in our check.
          </p>

          <p>
            For coffee, oats and baking, buyers report that both blends work, with the usual whey caveats: stir into warm, not boiling, liquids to avoid clumping, and expect cookie or candy inclusions to behave oddly in heat. Ghost's Coffee Ice Cream is the obvious coffee pick, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-coffee/">best protein powder for coffee</a> guide covers others that dissolve more cleanly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-ranges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do Ryse and Ghost Clear Whey and Vegan Protein Compare?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ghost has the stronger clear whey on paper, with 25 g of protein per scoop against about 22 g for Ryse Clear, per retailer listings at the time of writing. Both clear wheys are whey isolate mixed into a thin, juice-like drink in licensed fruit flavors, and both run around 100 calories per serving. Ghost's Clear Whey Orange Cream was $41.69 for 20 servings on Amazon when we checked, or about $2.08 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryse Clear leans on drink-brand licenses such as Kool-Aid and Country Time Lemonade, while Ghost's clear range uses its own fruit flavors like Blue Raspberry and Strawberry Watermelon. Clear whey suits hot weather and post-cardio drinks, but it does not work in coffee, oats or baking. For more options, see our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the vegan side, Ghost relaunched its Vegan Protein as V2 in early 2026 with about 25 to 27 g of protein per scoop from pea and pumpkin seed protein, per retailer listings. Ryse Loaded Plant Protein lists about 20 g from pea, chickpea and rice protein, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit, and was harder to find at the time of writing. If you are dairy-free and want a licensed-style dessert flavor, Ghost Vegan is the better-supplied option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each brand has a full review on the site with every range covered: read our <a href="/ryse-protein-powder-review/">Ryse protein powder review</a> and our <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a>. If you are weighing a mainstream whey instead of a licensed-flavor brand, our sibling head-to-head <a href="/premier-protein-vs-optimum-nutrition/">Premier Protein vs Optimum Nutrition</a> covers the two biggest big-box names.
          </p>
        </div>

