        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $54.99 Ghost tub and a $44.99 Ryse tub look $10 apart, but servings per tub vary by flavor. Divide the price by the number of 25 g servings. At the Amazon prices we saw, that is about $1.67 for Ryse and $2.12 for Ghost, a gap that grows with every tub you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor has the same label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands change the scoop size, sodium, sugar and even allergens between flavors. Ryse scoops range from about 30 g to over 37 g, and some Ryse flavors add soy lecithin or carrageenan. Read the panel on the exact flavor you order, not the one in a review or ad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying either as a lactose-free protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both are whey blends that include concentrate, which carries lactose. Ghost's added lactase may help mild sensitivity, but neither is a lactose-free product. If dairy reliably upsets your stomach, start with a whey isolate, a clear whey or a plant protein instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating licensed flavors as proof of quality</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Lucky Charms or Little Debbie logo tells you about the flavor license, not about purity or testing. Neither brand claims a sport certification. If quality assurance matters to you, look for a certification you can verify in the NSF or Informed Sport databases.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the 4 lb tub and sale cycles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryse's 4 lb tub cut the cost to about $1.50 per 25 g in our check, and both brands run frequent promotions. Buying the flavor you already know you like in the larger size, or during a sale, saves more than switching between these two brands.
              </p>
            </div>
